"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"][16]={"university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M16-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M16-L1 – Probability theory: diagnostic test and revision<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M16 – Transition: Bridge to the Master’s programme (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Transition · Supplementary/bridging\nrequirements<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M4, M2.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Determine conditional\nprobabilities, moments and the uncertainty of a mean without a formula\nsheet.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Diagnostic test: three different probability statements<\/h2>\n<p>Complete the tasks in 20 minutes without software. Record the\nassumptions behind each result. A diagnostic test is not a university\nentrance examination. It identifies which foundations for the upcoming\nMaster’s lessons are already in place.<\/p>\n<p>A. Production line A supplies 80% of the components and has a defect\nprobability of 0.01; line B supplies 20% with a defect probability of\n0.06. A randomly selected component is defective. What is the\nprobability that it came from B?<\/p>\n<p>B. The number\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof alerts in an observation window is 0, 1 or 2 with probabilities 0.2,\n0.5 and 0.3. Determine the expected value and variance.<\/p>\n<p>C. 100 independent, identically distributed measurements each have\nexpected value 12 and standard deviation 4. How variable is their mean?\nWhen is a normal approximation plausible?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions and common misconceptions<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001557_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Oben Produktionsanteile 800 und 200, unten Fehleranteile 8 und 12; unter Fehlern stammen 60 % von B.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Oben Produktionsanteile 800 und 200,\nunten Fehleranteile 8 und 12; unter Fehlern stammen 60 % von\nB.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Basisraten und bedingte Anteile.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>In A, let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenote a defect. Events A and B partition the sample space. By the\nproduct rule and the law of total probability,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.01<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.06<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.02<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(F)=0.8\\cdot0.01+0.2\\cdot0.06=0.02.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.06<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>0.02<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(B\\mid F)=\\frac{0.2\\cdot0.06}{0.02}=0.6.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The smaller line therefore accounts for more than half of the\ndefective components. It does not supply more than half of total\nproduction. The graphic distinguishes joint events from conditional\nproportions.<\/p>\n<p>In B,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[X]=0\\cdot0.2+1\\cdot0.5+2\\cdot0.3=1.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.7<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[X^2]=1.7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.7<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1.21<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.49<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(X)=1.7-1.21=0.49<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe standard deviation is 0.7 alerts; variance has squared units.<\/p>\n<p>In C,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mover><mi>X<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[\\bar X]=12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>X<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>16<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>100<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.16<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X)=16/100=0.16<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">SD<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>X<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{SD}(\\bar X)=0.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe central limit theorem for iid observations with finite positive\nvariance justifies a normal approximation asymptotically. One hundred\nobservations do not guarantee a good finite-sample approximation for\nhighly skewed distributions or rare events; dependence changes the\nvariance formula.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Transfer to modelling<\/h2>\n<p>A shared cause of defects leads to correlated measurements. In\ngeneral,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>X<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msubsup><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><\/msubsup><msubsup><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><\/msubsup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Cov<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X)=n^{-2}\\sum_{i=1}^n\\sum_{j=1}^n\\operatorname{Cov}(X_i,X_j)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith equal variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the same admissible pairwise correlation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ρ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis becomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma^2[1+(n-1)\\rho]/n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the example above, the variance is 1.744 rather than 0.16. More\nmeasurements are no substitute for controlling shared causes. This\nreasoning is needed in time series, hierarchical models and\nsampling.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assessment and revision<\/h2>\n<p>Award one point per task for the calculation method and one for the\nresult together with its assumptions. If you make mistakes in A, revise\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L1<\/a>\nand <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L6<\/a>;\nfor B, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L2<\/a>\nand <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L4<\/a>;\nfor C, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L7<\/a>.\nCheck your understanding with a new example: for equally sized lines\nwith defect rates 0.02 and 0.08,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(B\\mid F)=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWithout justification, a correct guess does not demonstrate mastery of\nthe method.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/principles-data-science/pages/3-4-probability-theory\">OpenStax:\nPrinciples of Data Science, 3.4 Probability Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · conditional\nprobability and Bayes<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://maths.dur.ac.uk/users/clare.wallace/MF/Prerequisites/some-probability-revision.html\">Durham\nUniversity: Mathematical Finance Prerequisites, Ch. 1<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · moments and\ncentral limit theorem<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M16-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M16-L2 – Statistical inference: diagnostic test and revision<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M16 – Transition: Bridge to the Master’s programme (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Transition · Supplementary/bridging\nrequirements<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M6, M4.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish estimation,\nintervals and tests; identify the consequences of sampling\nassumptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Diagnostic test: a series of measurements and a\ndecision<\/h2>\n<p>A sensor is read five times under controlled conditions: 8, 9, 10,\n11, 12 units. For tasks A and B, assume independent normally distributed\nmeasurements with unknown variance.<\/p>\n<p>A. Determine the mean, corrected sample variance and standard\nerror.<\/p>\n<p>B. Construct a two-sided 95% confidence interval and test\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>:<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0:\\mu=8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nagainst\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>:<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_1:\\mu\\ne8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat level 0.05. Use the reference value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0.975<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.77645<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_{4;0.975}=2.77645<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>C. A second working group examines ten independent null hypotheses,\neach at level 0.05, and reports only significant results. Calculate the\nrisk of at least one false alarm if all null hypotheses are true. How\ncan this risk be bounded without assuming independence?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked solution: uncertainty is part of the estimate<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001562_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Intervall 8,037 bis 11,963 um 10; der Nullwert 8 liegt knapp links außerhalb.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Intervall 8,037 bis 11,963 um 10; der\nNullwert 8 liegt knapp links außerhalb.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Intervall und zweiseitiger Test.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The deviations are −2, −1, 0, 1, 2. Hence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar x=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s^2=10/4=2.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>5<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.7071<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">SE=s/\\sqrt{5}=\\sqrt{0.5}\\approx0.7071<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDividing by four instead of five accounts for estimating the mean. The\nstandard deviation of individual values is not the same as the\nvariability of the estimator.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>±<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0.975<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>S<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>8.037<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>11.963<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=\\bar x\\pm t_{4;0.975}\\,SE\\approx[8.037;11.963].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><msqrt><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.828<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=\\frac{10-8}{\\sqrt{0.5}}\\approx2.828.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The absolute value exceeds the critical value 2.77645;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis narrowly rejected. The interval just excludes 8. In a frequentist\ninterval, probability refers to the long-run coverage of the procedure,\nnot to a random parameter in this particular interval once calculated.\nThe result may change if the model assumptions are violated.<\/p>\n<p>In C, the complementary probability is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mn>0.95<\/mn><mn>10<\/mn><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.4013<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-0.95^{10}\\approx0.4013<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBonferroni bounds the family-wise error rate using individual thresholds\nof\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.05<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.005<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.05/10=0.005<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\neven for dependent tests. This can be conservative. A test selected\nafter inspecting the data is not equivalent to a single test specified\nin advance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Transfer: statistical and practical significance<\/h2>\n<p>If only a change of three units were technically relevant, narrowly\nrejecting 8 would not demonstrate a sufficiently large change. This\nrequires a predefined practical threshold and a suitable study design.\nLikewise, failure to reject does not imply equality. An equivalence test\nrequires its own null hypothesis and justified tolerance limits. For\npaired observations, the dependence structure must be considered, for\nexample by analysing the differences.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assessment and revision<\/h2>\n<p>Award two points for each of the three tasks: correct method and\nresult with interpretation. If the choice of distribution or degrees of\nfreedom remains unclear, revise M6 (estimation, confidence intervals,\ntests); for multiple testing, revise the corresponding section in M6.\nCheck question: if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles while\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremains constant, the estimated standard error decreases by a factor of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/\\sqrt2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot by half. An interval does not automatically become exactly half as\nwide.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/prc/section2/prc221.htm\">NIST:\nConfidence interval approach, 7.2.2.1<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · t interval<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M16-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M16-L3 – Linear models: diagnostic test and revision<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M16 – Transition: Bridge to the Master’s programme (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Transition · Supplementary/bridging\nrequirements<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M8, M2.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Diagnose least squares,\nresiduals and identifiability using a small model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Diagnostic test: a calibration line<\/h2>\n<p>A test rig provides the synthetic pairs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,1),(1,4),(2,4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe first quantity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the setting, and the second\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the measurement. A. Determine the line\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat y=a+bx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nby least squares and calculate all residuals. B. Determine the residual\nsum of squares and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nC. An additional feature\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=2x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis to be included. Can the extended model with an intercept be estimated\nuniquely? What conclusion about causality does the line permit?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solution: projection rather than joining points<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001561_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Drei Messpunkte mit Residuen minus 0,5, plus 1 und minus 0,5 zur Anpassungsgeraden.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Drei Messpunkte mit Residuen minus 0,5,\nplus 1 und minus 0,5 zur Anpassungsgeraden.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Residuen einer Kalibriergeraden.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>We have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar x=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar y=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na centred sum of cross-products of 3 and a centred sum of squares for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof 2. Therefore,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/munder><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=\\frac{\\sum_i(x_i-\\bar x)(y_i-\\bar y)}{\\sum_i(x_i-\\bar x)^2}=1.5,\\qquad a=\\bar y-b\\bar x=1.5.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The predictions are 1.5; 3; 4.5. With the convention\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e_i=y_i-\\hat y_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe residuals are −0.5; 1; −0.5. Their sum is zero; also,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_i x_i e_i=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThese are the normal equations for an unweighted least-squares solution\nwith an intercept. The graphic shows the vertical distances to the line\n(not the geometrically shortest distances).<\/p>\n<p>The residual sum of squares is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{SSE}=1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the total sum of squares about the mean is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{SST}=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R^2=1-1.5/6=0.75<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis describes the fit to precisely these three data points. There is\nonly one residual degree of freedom; reliable statements about future\npredictive performance are unavailable.<\/p>\n<p>With the additional feature\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=2x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncolumns\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare linearly dependent. The slopes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b_x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b_z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nappear only as the sum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b_x+2b_z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nevery pair with this sum equal to 1.5 produces the same fitted values.\nResolving this requires changing the model or introducing a suitable\nadditional assumption. Ridge can select a unique solution if both slopes\nreceive a positive penalty, but it provides no additional empirical\ninformation about their separate effects.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Transfer: checking the model<\/h2>\n<p>Least squares does not require a normal distribution in order to\nminimise the sum of squares. Assumptions about the error structure\nbecome important for inferential statements. Residual plots can reveal\nnonlinearity and varying dispersion, but do not guarantee model\nvalidity. A slope is initially a conditional association; a causal\neffect requires, among other things, a suitable design or justified\nidentification assumptions. A high goodness-of-fit value does not rule\nout confounding.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assessment and revision<\/h2>\n<p>Two points per task: calculation or rank argument, and result with\ncorrect interpretation. If you make mistakes in A or B, revise <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L1<\/a>\nand residual analysis in M8. For C, revise linear dependence and rank\n(M2). Transfer question: if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis expressed in a new unit as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=10x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe slope in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>u<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbecomes 0.15 for the same model class; the intercept and fitted values\nremain unchanged.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/linear_model.html\">scikit-learn:\nLinear Models<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · least squares and ridge<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M16-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M16-L4 – Statistical learning: diagnostic test and revision<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M16 – Transition: Bridge to the Master’s programme (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Transition · Supplementary/bridging\nrequirements<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M10, M11, M14.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Recognise evaluation errors and\nassess a classifier by the relevant costs rather than accuracy\nalone.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Diagnostic test: detecting rare faults<\/h2>\n<p>A classifier for a fictitious facility is evaluated on 100\nindependent test cases. Ten are actual faults; the model detects eight\nof them, but also raises alarms in twelve fault-free cases. A. Determine\nthe confusion matrix, accuracy, precision and recall. B. A model that\nnever raises an alarm achieves 90% accuracy. Which alternative is\npreferable if a missed fault costs 20 and a false alarm costs 1, while\ncorrect decisions have no cost? C. A developer normalises all 500\nobservations before splitting the data, selects the best model using the\ntest set and then reports its test score again. What errors have\noccurred and how would you design the procedure?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solution: matrix and decision loss<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001560_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Konfusionsmatrix mit TP 8, FN 2, FP 12 und TN 78; Gesamtverlust 52.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Konfusionsmatrix mit TP 8, FN 2, FP 12\nund TN 78; Gesamtverlust 52.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Seltene Störungen und Fehlentscheidungen.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The four entries are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">TP=8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">FN=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">FP=12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>78<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">TN=78<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the matrix shown in the graphic, rows denote ground truth and columns\ndenote decisions. The metrics are<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>78<\/mn><\/mrow><mn>100<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.86<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>8<\/mn><mn>20<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>8<\/mn><mn>10<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Accuracy}=\\frac{8+78}{100}=0.86,\\qquad \\mathrm{Precision}=\\frac8{20}=0.4,\\qquad \\mathrm{Recall}=\\frac8{10}=0.8.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The harmonic mean of precision and recall is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>15<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.5333<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_1=2\\cdot0.4\\cdot0.8/(0.4+0.8)=8/15\\approx0.5333<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a model with no predicted positives, precision is mathematically\nundefined; software must specify a convention. Accuracy alone favours\nthe trivial solution here.<\/p>\n<p>Under the assumed costs, the alarm model has a total loss of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>52<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\cdot20+12\\cdot1=52<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the model without alarms has a loss of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\cdot20=200<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUnder these cost assumptions, choose the alarm model; other costs or\ndistributions could change the decision. Given a correctly calibrated\nfault probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nraising an alarm is optimal when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-p&lt;20p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthat is,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>21<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p&gt;1/21<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(a tie at the threshold). The threshold is not a parameter to optimise\nusing the 100 test cases.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solution: reliable validation<\/h2>\n<p>Normalising before splitting uses information from the later test\nvalues; the subsequent model selection based on the test set compromises\nthe final evaluation again. Split the data first. Within the development\nset, fit scaling, feature selection and training separately for each\nfold, using only the corresponding training portion. Choose the model\nconfiguration according to a predefined target metric, refit the\nselected pipeline on the development set and evaluate it exactly once on\nthe untouched test set. For temporal or group dependence, choose\nappropriate splitting rules. Nested cross-validation is an alternative\nwhen the selection procedure itself is to be evaluated.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assessment and revision<\/h2>\n<p>Two points per task for the correct approach and a justified answer.\nRevise M10 if you make mistakes in metrics and validation, M11 for model\ncomparison and M14 for training neural models. Additional task: with the\nsame sensitivity of 0.8 and false positive rate of 12/90, but a fault\nprevalence of only 1%, precision is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.008<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.008<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.132<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.0571<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.008/(0.008+0.132)\\approx0.0571<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe prevalence of the target class is therefore essential when\ninterpreting results in deployment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/common_pitfalls\">scikit-learn:\nCommon pitfalls and recommended practices<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · data leakage<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/cross_validation\">scikit-learn:\nCross-validation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · validation and temporal splits<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M16-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M16-L5 – Intensive revision of numerical methods, optimisation and linear algebra<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M16 – Transition: Bridge to the Master’s programme (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Transition · Supplementary/bridging\nrequirements<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M2, M9, M14.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Assess conditioning, gradients\nand step size, and distinguish numerical stability from computational\nspeed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Diagnostic test: direction and scaling<\/h2>\n<p>A. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">diag<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.01<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\operatorname{diag}(1,0.01)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.01<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=(1,0.01)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ndetermine the solution to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Ax=b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe 2-norm condition number and the solution after changing the second\ncomponent of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto 0.02. B. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(u,v)=\\tfrac12(u^2+9v^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncalculate the gradient and two steps of gradient descent from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith learning rate 0.1. Which fixed positive learning rates converge\nfrom every starting point? C. Why should one not routinely form an\nexplicit inverse when solving linear systems and least-squares\nproblems?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions: conditioning and methods<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001558_en.svg\"\nalt=\"u fällt pro Schritt auf 90 Prozent, v auf 10 Prozent seines vorherigen Werts; nach zwei Schritten 1,62 und 0,02.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">u fällt pro Schritt auf 90 Prozent, v auf\n10 Prozent seines vorherigen Werts; nach zwei Schritten 1,62 und\n0,02.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Zwei Richtungen, eine Lernrate.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The initial solution is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,1)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nafter perturbation it becomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,2)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe singular values of the diagonal matrix are 1 and 0.01, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa_2(A)=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA small absolute perturbation of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan cause a large change in the solution along the poorly conditioned\ndirection. This is a property of the problem, not solely of the solver\nused.<\/p>\n<p>The gradient is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mi>v<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla f=(u,9v)^T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe update can be written component by component:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>u<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mi>η<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_{k+1}=(1-\\eta)u_k,\\qquad v_{k+1}=(1-9\\eta)v_k.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe points are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1.8<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1.8;0.2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1.62<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>0.02<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1.62;0.02)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe objective values decrease from 20 to 1.8 and 1.314. Both factors\nmust have absolute values less than one. The common range is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;\\eta&lt;2/9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=2/9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe magnitude of the second component remains unchanged; with a larger\nstep size it can grow. The graphic illustrates the striking difference\nbetween the two directions.<\/p>\n<p>For numerical solution, a suitable factorisation is useful, for\nexample; it avoids explicitly computing the entire inverse. In\nleast-squares problems, QR or SVD can offer advantages over forming\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A^TA<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nwith full column rank,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>κ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa_2(A^TA)=\\kappa_2(A)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis can exacerbate the amplification of rounding errors. Conditioning\nand algorithmic stability are distinct considerations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Computational check<\/h2>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>A <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.diag([<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.01<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(np.linalg.solve(A, [<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.01<\/span>]), [<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(np.linalg.solve(A, [<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.02<\/span>]), [<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(np.linalg.cond(A), <span class=\"fl\">100.<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> _ <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    x <span class=\"op\">-=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.1<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">9.<\/span>]) <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> x<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(x, [<span class=\"fl\">1.62<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.02<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(x)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>When testing your own gradient, the central difference\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(f(x+he_i)-f(x-he_i))/(2h)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan serve as a reference. However, excessively small\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\namplifies cancellation and rounding errors; excessively large\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndistorts the local derivative. Check several step sizes rather than just\none.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assessment and revision<\/h2>\n<p>Two points per task. For A or C, revise matrix factorisation and\nconditioning in M2/M9; for B, gradient methods in M9 and M14. Transfer:\nif the second curvature increases from 9 to 100, the common convergence\nrange shrinks to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0.02<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;\\eta&lt;0.02<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis explains the importance of scaling and preconditioning without\nclaiming a universally optimal learning rate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://numpy.org/doc/stable/reference/routines.linalg.html?highlight=numpy\">NumPy:\nLinear algebra<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · numerical solution methods<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M16-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M16-L6 – Programming and research diagnostics with further study paths<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M16 – Transition: Bridge to the Master’s programme (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Transition · Supplementary/bridging\nrequirements<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M3, M7, M15.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Assess data validation,\nreproducible code and research design as interrelated prerequisites.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Diagnostic test: a small time series<\/h2>\n<p>The table contains daily counts from a synthetic device test. The\norder is chronological; days 1–4 are for development and days 5–6 for\ntesting. A missing development value is to be replaced by the mean of\nthe observed development values. The evaluation predicts the next day\nusing the mean of the last two preprocessed values.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Day<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Count<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">missing<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">8<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">6<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">10<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">6<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">12<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>A. Determine the replacement value and predictions for days 5 and 6\n(the actual value on day 5 is revealed only after its prediction).\nCalculate MAE. B. Write a function with input validation and at least\ntwo tests. C. Design a fair comparison for a research question and list\nthe information another person needs to reproduce it.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solution: chronological data boundaries<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001563_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Sechs Tage; Ersatzwert 6 aus Tag 1–4, Prognosen 7 und 8 für Tag 5 und 6, freigegebene Werte 10 und 12.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Sechs Tage; Ersatzwert 6 aus Tag 1–4,\nPrognosen 7 und 8 für Tag 5 und 6, freigegebene Werte 10 und\n12.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Chronologische Prognose und Auswertung.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The development mean is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(4+8+6)/3=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe method predicts day 5 as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(8+6)/2=7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand day 6 as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(6+10)/2=8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe absolute errors are 3 and 4; the MAE is 3.5 items. Day 5 is used\nonly as known history for day 6, not for the estimate of day 5. If both\ndays were forecast at the same time, this protocol would be invalid. A\nreplacement value calculated from the entire series would be 8 and would\nincorporate future information. Within the development phase, the same\ntemporal separation rule applies whenever historical validation is\nsimulated.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reference implementation<\/h2>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> statistics <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> mean<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> math <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> isfinite<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> forecast(train, future):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> series <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (train, future):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">any<\/span>(x <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> <span class=\"va\">None<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> (<span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> isfinite(x) <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span> x <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>               <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> series):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&quot;invalid count&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    known <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [x <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> train <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> x <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> <span class=\"va\">None<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(train) <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> known <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> future:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&quot;insufficient data&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">any<\/span>(x <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"va\">None<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> future):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&quot;test targets missing&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    fill <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> mean(known)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    hist <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [fill <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> x <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"va\">None<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> x <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> train]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    predictions <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> []<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> observed <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> future:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        predictions.append(mean(hist[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>:]))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        hist.append(observed)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> predictions, mean(<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(y<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>p) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> y,p <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">zip<\/span>(future,predictions))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> forecast([<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,<span class=\"va\">None<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>]) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> ([<span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>],<span class=\"fl\">3.5<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> forecast([<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> ([<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>],<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-24\"><a href=\"#cb1-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">try<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-25\"><a href=\"#cb1-25\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    forecast([<span class=\"va\">None<\/span>,<span class=\"va\">None<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-26\"><a href=\"#cb1-26\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">except<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-27\"><a href=\"#cb1-27\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">pass<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-28\"><a href=\"#cb1-28\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">else<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-29\"><a href=\"#cb1-29\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">AssertionError<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&quot;missing guard&quot;<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The function accepts finite non-negative numbers; for actual count\ndata, integer values and unique timestamps may also need to be checked.\nThe examples test one particular path and one boundary case; they do not\nreplace comprehensive testing.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Research diagnostics and further study path<\/h2>\n<p>For example, compare the two-day moving-average forecast with the\nlast observed value, using the same time boundaries and metric. The\nbaseline predicts 6 and 10, with an MAE of 3; it wins in this tiny test.\nThis does not demonstrate its general superiority. Document data\nprovenance, hash, splits, method, code version, environment, run\ncommand, metric and complete results. Specify expected results as tests\nwithout deriving them from test data for model selection.<\/p>\n<p>Two points per task. For data validation and testing, revise M3/M7;\nfor research design, M15. Transfer: if two consecutive measurements are\ncollected per device, the device identifier must not be spread across\ntraining and testing when evaluating on previously unseen devices. The\nsplit strategy always follows the actual deployment question.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/common_pitfalls\">scikit-learn:\nCommon pitfalls and recommended practices<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · data leakage<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/cross_validation\">scikit-learn:\nCross-validation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · validation and temporal splits<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M16-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M16-L7 – Proofs, complexity and theoretical algorithms: entry diagnostics<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M16 – Transition: Bridge to the Master’s programme (7 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Transition · Supplementary/diagnostic<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M2, M3, M13.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Understand proofs, asymptotic\nbounds and theoretical algorithms as prerequisites for advanced computer\nscience.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Diagnostic test: from example to proof<\/h2>\n<p>A. Prove that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>k<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_{k=1}^n(2k-1)=n^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor all natural numbers\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ge1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nB. A procedure compares every unordered pair among\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nobjects exactly once. State the number of comparisons and its tight\nasymptotic bound. Another procedure halves a set of size\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=2^m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nstill to be investigated at each iteration until its size is one. How\nmany halvings are required? C. Explain whether polynomial time for\nverifying a proposed SAT certificate already provides a polynomial-time\nalgorithm for finding a certificate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solution: induction and computational cost<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001559_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Vier anwachsende Quadrate mit 1, 4, 9 und 16 Punkten; neu hinzugefügte Ränder enthalten 1, 3, 5 und 7 Punkte.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Vier anwachsende Quadrate mit 1, 4, 9 und\n16 Punkten; neu hinzugefügte Ränder enthalten 1, 3, 5 und 7\nPunkte.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Ungerade Summanden bilden Quadrate.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe statement holds because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\cdot1-1=1=1^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAssume it holds for an arbitrary\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ge1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAdding the next summand gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munderover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>k<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_{k=1}^{n+1}(2k-1)=n^2+2(n+1)-1=(n+1)^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The statement is thus proved by induction. Checking the first ten\nnumbers alone would not constitute proof for all natural numbers. The\ngraphic visualises the identity by incrementally extending square dot\npatterns; algebraic induction validates it independently of the\ndrawing.<\/p>\n<p>In B, there are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n(n-1)/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npairs. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ge2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n^2/4\\le n(n-1)/2\\le n^2/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHence the number of comparisons is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Theta(n^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe halving procedure requires\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=\\log_2 n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsteps. The runtime is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Theta(\\log n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nonly if each iteration requires constant work; merely halving the\nproblem size is insufficient. If a list is copied each time, the copying\ncost must be included.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solution: certificates and search problems<\/h2>\n<p>A propositional formula in conjunctive normal form can be checked for\na given assignment by ensuring that every clause contains at least one\ntrue literal. With an appropriate input model, this takes polynomial\ntime in the input length. The class NP comprises decision problems with\npolynomial-time verifiable certificates of polynomially bounded length\nfor yes-instances. SAT belongs to NP and is NP-complete. The class P\ncomprises decision problems solvable deterministically in polynomial\ntime. Whether P and NP coincide is an open question. Efficient\nverification alone does not yield any known polynomial-time SAT search\nalgorithm. For SAT, the decision and search versions can be related in\npolynomial time by fixing variables one by one, assuming a\npolynomial-time decision algorithm is available.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assessment and final diagnostics<\/h2>\n<p>Two points per task: sound justification and correct result. For A,\nrevise proof techniques and discrete mathematics; for B, algorithm\nanalysis (M3 and the computer science course); for C, logic and SAT\n(M13). Transfer: the statement ‘Every quadratic algorithm takes at least\none second’ does not follow from the asymptotic bound; hardware,\nconstants and input size remain relevant.<\/p>\n<p>After M16, summarise all seven self-tests as a profile of 0–6 points\neach. 5–6 with traceable assumptions: proceed directly; 3–4: targeted\nrevision; 0–2: work on the foundations first. These thresholds are\nteaching suggestions, not an empirically validated test or a formal\nadmission rule. A good overall result is no substitute for targeted\nrevision of a weak dimension. L1–L3 are particularly important for\nadvanced statistics; optimisation and deep learning require L5, and\ntheoretical AI additionally requires L7. L4 and L6 are relevant to all\nadvanced modelling topics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-1200j-mathematics-for-computer-science-spring-2024/pages/readings/\">MIT\nOCW: Mathematics for Computer Science, Readings<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · proofs, induction,\nasymptotic analysis; chapter listing opened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-404j-theory-of-computation-fall-2020/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOCW: Theory of Computation, Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · P, NP, SAT,\nNP-completeness; search snippet only, notes not checked<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
